WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.610 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:84%
The following
content is provided

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.340 align:middle line:84%
by MIT OpenCourseWare under
a Creative Commons license.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:10.120 align:middle line:84%
Additional information about our
license and MIT OpenCourseWare,

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.730 align:middle line:84%
in general, is available
at OCW.MIT.edu.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:22.510 align:middle line:90%


00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:25.870 align:middle line:84%
GEORGE CHURCH: OK,
so welcome tonight,

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:30.050 align:middle line:84%
a very special lecture for
me tonight in more ways

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:31.540 align:middle line:90%
than I can express.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.760 align:middle line:84%
This is the last of my
lectures, but I look forward

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.490 align:middle line:90%
to your lectures coming up.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:37.190 align:middle line:90%
I really do.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.010 align:middle line:84%
I've heard from the
TFs, and from what

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.150 align:middle line:84%
I've seen from talking
to many of you,

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.530 align:middle line:84%
this is going to be a really
amazing set of projects

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.590 align:middle line:84%
this year, more
than any other year.

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:55.360 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, last week, we asked what
can biology do for computing?

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.290 align:middle line:84%
And this week is going to be
a little less nuts and bolts

00:00:59.290 --> 00:00:59.980 align:middle line:90%
than usual.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.870 align:middle line:84%
The entire course--
as you all understand,

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.269 align:middle line:84%
you're all self-selected
to life survey

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:08.050 align:middle line:84%
courses, at least this one,
because you're still here.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:11.906 align:middle line:84%
And tonight's going to be more
of a survey than ever before,

00:01:11.906 --> 00:01:14.860 align:middle line:84%
and it's going to be talking
about topics for which we don't

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:19.450 align:middle line:90%
have answers for the most part.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:23.080 align:middle line:84%
There'll be some interesting
details along the way.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:27.040 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, instead of what
biology can do for computing,

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.560 align:middle line:84%
this is more about what
biology and computing can

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:30.670 align:middle line:90%
do for the world.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.800 align:middle line:84%
That's really what
we want to do,

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.410 align:middle line:84%
and also, the
higher level network

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:39.250 align:middle line:84%
models that we can build since
this is network number three.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:40.710 align:middle line:90%
So we'll start out--

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.440 align:middle line:84%
we've been talking mainly about
cellular models as our highest

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:45.940 align:middle line:84%
level of networking,
and so we'll

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.160 align:middle line:84%
talk about multicellular
models, in particular,

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:52.180 align:middle line:84%
the sort of multicellular
models that you get that involve

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:59.110 align:middle line:84%
sensory integration and
integration of higher levels,

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:03.220 align:middle line:84%
and then all the way up to
multi-organ systems, where

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:07.120 align:middle line:84%
we'll take an example
from the atomic scale,

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.890 align:middle line:84%
all the way up to
organ system failure.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.740 align:middle line:90%
And then we'll go from there.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.500 align:middle line:84%
So let's start with
multicellular models.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:21.880 align:middle line:90%
And this is something that I--

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.310 align:middle line:84%
I'll show you a few slides
that will remind you

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.592 align:middle line:90%
of the first lecture.

00:02:25.592 --> 00:02:27.550 align:middle line:84%
They either were taken
from the first lecture--

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:30.980 align:middle line:84%
in this case, this is a wildly
different author, and approach,

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:32.962 align:middle line:84%
and so forth, but
it's the same lesson

00:02:32.962 --> 00:02:34.420 align:middle line:84%
as from the first
lecture, which is

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.610 align:middle line:84%
we not only have these
exponential curves going

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.800 align:middle line:84%
through the 1900s, but
they're super exponential.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.480 align:middle line:84%
They go up, and the
trend lines you get

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.690 align:middle line:84%
depend on which set of points
you use, the steepest one being

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:46.000 align:middle line:90%
the most recent one, 1995.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.740 align:middle line:84%
And this basically
is part of when

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.590 align:middle line:84%
it is that we will
have computing power

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:56.260 align:middle line:84%
that might be on the order of
an integrated human neural net.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:59.020 align:middle line:84%
We talked about neural nets
last time as a metaphor,

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:02.260 align:middle line:84%
or as an algorithm,
but when will

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:05.530 align:middle line:84%
we actually have a
computer that has

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:10.930 align:middle line:84%
the capability of the
human cerebral cortex,

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.120 align:middle line:84%
or entire integrated
nervous system?

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.520 align:middle line:84%
And the basis of
those calculations

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.480 align:middle line:84%
that Moravec made,
and in lecture one

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:22.030 align:middle line:84%
Kurzweil was the
super exponential

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:23.380 align:middle line:90%
curve that I showed.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:26.710 align:middle line:84%
Moravec made them
based on his studies

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:29.540 align:middle line:90%
on how the retina works.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:33.700 align:middle line:84%
And I think those
of you who have

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.520 align:middle line:84%
done a lot of
computer programming,

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:40.750 align:middle line:84%
that understanding
how the retina works

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:42.880 align:middle line:84%
is probably one of
the more intuitive

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.070 align:middle line:84%
of our various human senses, in
the sense that you can program

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:51.920 align:middle line:84%
an algorithm that might do in
the lower right of slide four

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.370 align:middle line:90%
here, edge or motion detection.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:59.140 align:middle line:84%
When a frog sees a fly, black
and white, high contrast,

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:03.340 align:middle line:84%
zoom across its visual
field, something goes off.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.830 align:middle line:84%
And you could write an
algorithm that could do that,

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:10.130 align:middle line:84%
given a few frames
of any kind of form.

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:13.880 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, but there's been
literature on the subject.

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:16.990 align:middle line:84%
And when you go through even
the best algorithms right now

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:19.420 align:middle line:84%
for doing these
kind of calculations

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:24.460 align:middle line:84%
and you figure out how
long it takes to do--

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:29.080 align:middle line:84%
the retina is doing about 10
million detections per second.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:35.020 align:middle line:84%
And then you scale up from
that 0.02 gram chunk of retina

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:39.070 align:middle line:84%
up to the 1,500
gram human brain,

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:42.680 align:middle line:84%
then you get the extrapolation
that was in the previous slide.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:44.963 align:middle line:84%
I think this is interesting
both as an introduction

00:04:44.963 --> 00:04:47.380 align:middle line:84%
to the kind of algorithms we'd
like to think about when we

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.950 align:middle line:84%
think about integrating
a multicellular system,

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:55.450 align:middle line:84%
but also in terms of
where the compute power

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:58.510 align:middle line:84%
both biological and
computational is going.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:02.500 align:middle line:84%
Do we want to engineer
biological systems

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:07.270 align:middle line:84%
to keep up with these silicon
or whatever other computational

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:13.040 align:middle line:84%
system we're using a
decade or so from now.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.290 align:middle line:84%
Now, so I said that
the visual system

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:20.230 align:middle line:84%
is a relatively intuitive
computational system

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:21.590 align:middle line:90%
to program.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:25.370 align:middle line:84%
In contrast, I would say
the olfactory system is less

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:27.980 align:middle line:90%
intuitive, at least for me.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:31.340 align:middle line:84%
We know now quite a bit
about the molecular biology

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:31.940 align:middle line:90%
of the system.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:33.410 align:middle line:90%
There are 1,000 receptors.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:38.450 align:middle line:84%
It's probably one of the largest
classes of genomic repeated

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:40.160 align:middle line:90%
proteins.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.100 align:middle line:84%
And these receptors
are in the same family

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.800 align:middle line:84%
as the receptors that are
the major class of drug

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:49.370 align:middle line:84%
receptors, the G protein
coupled receptors.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:52.100 align:middle line:84%
And there is
basically one receptor

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.080 align:middle line:84%
per cell, which is
quite a trick, right?

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:56.960 align:middle line:84%
Because you got one receptor
from mom and one from dad.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.240 align:middle line:84%
And you've got
thousands of receptors.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:02.540 align:middle line:84%
But there's basically
one per cell.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:10.340 align:middle line:84%
And they detect olfactory
molecule concentrations

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:21.500 align:middle line:84%
over about seven logs, where
they can detect a concentration

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:26.240 align:middle line:84%
threshold with a particular
standard deviation.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:28.040 align:middle line:84%
Now this is the
beginnings of the model,

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:30.650 align:middle line:84%
just stating the fact
that you know about it.

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:35.225 align:middle line:84%
When you look at the
neuroanatomy, you've got this--

00:06:35.225 --> 00:06:37.100 align:middle line:84%
the odorant molecules
down at the very bottom

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:41.900 align:middle line:84%
of this figure, cilia
olfactory neurons, which is

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.630 align:middle line:90%
a primary signal transduction.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:48.740 align:middle line:84%
This thing goes up where it's
integrated in the glomeruli.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:50.980 align:middle line:84%
And you can think of
this like the neural nets

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:52.688 align:middle line:84%
that we're talking
about last time, where

00:06:52.688 --> 00:06:54.200 align:middle line:90%
you had these interneurons.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:07:01.280 align:middle line:84%
You had the extra layer that
we showed was so important.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.350 align:middle line:84%
And the thing that's
amazing about this system

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:09.290 align:middle line:84%
are lodged in these
four basic olfactory

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:13.100 align:middle line:84%
facts, which I take from
Hopfield's work, which

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:16.730 align:middle line:84%
is really just like this is the
same Hopfield that introduced

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:20.540 align:middle line:84%
us to or really championed
the concept of neural nets

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:23.900 align:middle line:84%
as a computational
algorithmic metaphor.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:30.410 align:middle line:84%
And here, the idea is you have
odor and memory recognition.

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:33.110 align:middle line:84%
You have background
elimination, just

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:38.540 align:middle line:84%
like how your eyes adapt
to a mostly red room,

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:42.410 align:middle line:84%
your nose is great, or
whole olfactory system

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:47.210 align:middle line:84%
is great at adapting
to background odorants.

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:51.830 align:middle line:84%
So you have one known and
one unknown thoroughly mixed.

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:54.560 align:middle line:84%
Number three, you can
have component separation.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.680 align:middle line:84%
You can have a few
different odors at once.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:03.215 align:middle line:84%
You have odor separation
where you have unknowns.

00:08:03.215 --> 00:08:06.530 align:middle line:90%


00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:10.770 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the combination of
these is quite remarkable.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:18.590 align:middle line:84%
So the basis of the model that
Hopfield has proposed is this.

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:22.970 align:middle line:84%
You have a coverage for
each of the i receptors,

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:25.070 align:middle line:90%
where i goes from 1 to 1,000.

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.080 align:middle line:84%
This will vary depending
on the organism.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:30.390 align:middle line:84%
Mice have twice as many as
we do, probably, and so on.

00:08:30.390 --> 00:08:36.530 align:middle line:84%
But the coverage of
each of those receptors

00:08:36.530 --> 00:08:45.050 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the minimum
coverage for the concentration

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:47.540 align:middle line:84%
of the target, where let's
say the target is the target

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:52.010 align:middle line:84%
odorant that you're looking for
over the concentration that's

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:55.880 align:middle line:84%
the threshold for firing
that neuron for that target,

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:57.500 align:middle line:90%
threshold of t.

00:08:57.500 --> 00:09:01.220 align:middle line:84%
And you multiply that times
either one or some fraction.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:05.150 align:middle line:84%
It's 1 if you really
hit the target.

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:09.620 align:middle line:84%
And it's this fraction for
that particular receptor

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.150 align:middle line:90%
and that particular target.

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.620 align:middle line:90%
You've got some crosstalk.

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:15.260 align:middle line:84%
And the amount of
crosstalk you can

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:21.030 align:middle line:84%
think of this as a field
of receptors, which

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:23.090 align:middle line:90%
all have variable binding.

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:27.210 align:middle line:84%
And that binding is over
the six or seven logs here.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:29.135 align:middle line:84%
And this just reflects
that same thing

00:09:29.135 --> 00:09:30.260 align:middle line:90%
that we were talking about.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:32.960 align:middle line:90%


00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:37.220 align:middle line:84%
So the total number
of inputs going

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:40.190 align:middle line:84%
to the next layer in the
neural net, the coverage here,

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:47.520 align:middle line:84%
is the sum of this target signal
plus the background signal.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:48.770 align:middle line:90%
So the first term is target.

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:50.760 align:middle line:84%
And the right hand
term is the background.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:53.720 align:middle line:84%
And so you can see they have the
same form, the same threshold,

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:57.425 align:middle line:84%
and concentration. c sub b is
concentration of background.

00:09:57.425 --> 00:10:03.630 align:middle line:90%


00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:07.290 align:middle line:84%
Now, so let's see
how this plays out

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:13.800 align:middle line:84%
when you actually try this
out in an olfactory processing

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:14.460 align:middle line:90%
problem.

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:17.490 align:middle line:90%
Here you have an odor space.

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:23.160 align:middle line:84%
And what you have here
is 80 different neurons

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:26.790 align:middle line:84%
is the y-axis at the
top of slide seven.

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:33.720 align:middle line:84%
And then across the x-axis
is time in milliseconds going

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.370 align:middle line:90%
from 0 to 800 milliseconds.

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:41.040 align:middle line:84%
And this is all modeled on
fairly realistic parameters

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.740 align:middle line:90%
based on experiments.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:46.590 align:middle line:84%
And so what you have here
is 80 adapting neurons.

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:49.260 align:middle line:84%
And what you're doing is
two sniffs of a mixed odor.

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:52.920 align:middle line:84%
That's what this
is modeling, OK?

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:59.490 align:middle line:84%
First, you have a 100 to 500
milliseconds, the range from--

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:02.820 align:middle line:84%
so you can see that something's
happening at 100 milliseconds.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:05.160 align:middle line:84%
And up to 500 you
have a mixed odor,

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:09.330 align:middle line:84%
which is 50 parts of x
plus 1,000 parts of y,

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:14.550 align:middle line:90%
or 1,000 times y.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:18.180 align:middle line:84%
And then 500 milliseconds
you change this ratio very

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:19.140 align:middle line:90%
slightly, you see?

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:27.350 align:middle line:84%
You just up x by 75 and
y up from 1,000 to 1,100.

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:33.410 align:middle line:84%
OK, so that's the paradigm,
50 and 1,000 to 75 and 1,100.

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:37.340 align:middle line:84%
And the sniff at 100
milliseconds, that first one,

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:42.690 align:middle line:84%
that mixed odorant, it activates
more than half the neurons.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:45.170 align:middle line:84%
So this is not like
your retina, where

00:11:45.170 --> 00:11:47.180 align:middle line:84%
if you have a
single point source

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:52.070 align:middle line:84%
it will activate that one
broad cell, or maybe a center

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:54.380 align:middle line:90%
surround effect.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:57.830 align:middle line:84%
But it's really activating
half of the neurons, OK?

00:11:57.830 --> 00:12:00.920 align:middle line:84%
And then the changed sniff at
500 milliseconds-- remember,

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:02.990 align:middle line:90%
just these two small changes--

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:06.350 align:middle line:90%
is almost invisible, right?

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:11.720 align:middle line:84%
So that's what you're
seeing right here,

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:16.400 align:middle line:84%
OK, is this huge,
swamping amount.

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:21.950 align:middle line:84%
But then in B-- so that's
what happened in A. B,

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:26.660 align:middle line:84%
you've now plotting instead
of individual neurons,

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:30.470 align:middle line:84%
you're doing the instantaneous
rate in hertz, OK?

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:38.210 align:middle line:84%
And now, you can see
the second sniff here

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:41.060 align:middle line:84%
at the 500 milliseconds,
which even though you really

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:42.842 align:middle line:84%
can't see it in the
corresponding point

00:12:42.842 --> 00:12:45.050 align:middle line:84%
where you're looking at all
these individual neurons.

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:51.320 align:middle line:84%
And so you can see that
even a 20% spread in one

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:56.870 align:middle line:84%
of these parameters is enough
to get this kind of easily

00:12:56.870 --> 00:12:59.270 align:middle line:90%
detectable signal.

00:12:59.270 --> 00:13:01.730 align:middle line:84%
Now, that's an example
of a simulation.

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:04.850 align:middle line:84%
That's not a simulation of
a particular experimental

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:07.910 align:middle line:84%
dataset, but it's based
on experimental datasets.

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:09.830 align:middle line:84%
And the code, this
is not Mathematica,

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:12.050 align:middle line:84%
this is Matlab,
which we all wish

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:14.930 align:middle line:84%
we could have interchangeably
taught in this course.

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:17.210 align:middle line:84%
And you can see here in
green are the comments.

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:18.680 align:middle line:84%
And you can see
some of the things

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:20.138 align:middle line:84%
that we've been
talking about here.

00:13:20.138 --> 00:13:23.673 align:middle line:90%


00:13:23.673 --> 00:13:25.340 align:middle line:84%
The number of receptor
types is actually

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:27.380 align:middle line:90%
here 2,000 instead of 1,000.

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:29.720 align:middle line:84%
You see the target,
remember, I said

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:33.715 align:middle line:84%
could be some random number
that goes over six logarithms.

00:13:33.715 --> 00:13:35.090 align:middle line:84%
Well, the way
that's done here is

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:39.718 align:middle line:84%
here's a random number
generated and you generate

00:13:39.718 --> 00:13:40.760 align:middle line:90%
as the log of the target.

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:43.052 align:middle line:84%
And then, you get the target
by taking the exponential.

00:13:43.052 --> 00:13:47.880 align:middle line:84%
It's very straightforward and
the code goes on from there.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:52.760 align:middle line:84%
OK, now this brings us to
a very interesting point.

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:55.382 align:middle line:84%
Since these three
lectures on networks,

00:13:55.382 --> 00:13:57.090 align:middle line:84%
and in a certain sense,
the whole course,

00:13:57.090 --> 00:14:01.250 align:middle line:84%
is building towards systems
biology network models.

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:03.170 align:middle line:84%
And for these
models to be useful,

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:05.002 align:middle line:84%
in the same sense that
the models we have

00:14:05.002 --> 00:14:06.710 align:middle line:84%
for X-ray crystallography,
and the models

00:14:06.710 --> 00:14:09.320 align:middle line:84%
we've had for genome
interpretation in terms

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:12.740 align:middle line:84%
of homology, and
sequence folding,

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:16.460 align:middle line:84%
and so forth needed
to be shared.

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:18.410 align:middle line:84%
The models, even
more than the data,

00:14:18.410 --> 00:14:22.640 align:middle line:84%
need to be shareable
because in a certain sense,

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:25.580 align:middle line:84%
even modest
manipulation of the data

00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:27.903 align:middle line:84%
usually involves some
good model behind it.

00:14:27.903 --> 00:14:29.570 align:middle line:84%
And so these are some
of the work groups

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:32.840 align:middle line:84%
that are working on
different modeling schemes.

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:35.375 align:middle line:84%
In particular, I
should point out

00:14:35.375 --> 00:14:38.570 align:middle line:84%
that BioSpice is
now DARPA BioSpice,

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:41.570 align:middle line:84%
and SBML is kind of a
growing part of that.

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:45.230 align:middle line:84%
System Biology Markup Language
is a way of sharing this data.

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:48.830 align:middle line:84%
And as yet, there's not
the kind of convention

00:14:48.830 --> 00:14:52.070 align:middle line:84%
that we have in
crystallography and DNA

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:53.888 align:middle line:90%
sequencing where you submit--

00:14:53.888 --> 00:14:55.430 align:middle line:84%
at the time that
you publish a paper,

00:14:55.430 --> 00:14:59.840 align:middle line:84%
you submit your data
and model, data/model,

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:02.420 align:middle line:84%
with an accession number
to the database or else

00:15:02.420 --> 00:15:03.560 align:middle line:90%
you don't get it published.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:05.990 align:middle line:84%
We're nowhere near that now
for the rest of biology.

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:08.210 align:middle line:84%
But these kind of
efforts, probably,

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:09.480 align:middle line:90%
are moving in that direction.

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:12.595 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that the
features of each of these

00:15:12.595 --> 00:15:13.970 align:middle line:84%
are some of the
things that we've

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:15.053 align:middle line:90%
been talking about before.

00:15:15.053 --> 00:15:18.980 align:middle line:84%
You've got stochastic
modeling, kinetic modeling,

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:30.350 align:middle line:84%
enzyme receptor cell geometry,
neural nets, and so on.

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:32.310 align:middle line:84%
This is by no means
comprehensive.

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:34.310 align:middle line:84%
And there are hypertext
links here if you really

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:35.690 align:middle line:90%
want to dig into them.

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:37.520 align:middle line:84%
And some of the
platforms that they've

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:42.260 align:middle line:84%
been thinking about different
ways of making it slightly less

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:43.430 align:middle line:90%
system-dependent.

00:15:43.430 --> 00:15:45.530 align:middle line:90%
Everyone has their attempts.

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:50.420 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, Windows is
not system-independent.

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:53.420 align:middle line:84%
So that's two very
brief examples

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:58.970 align:middle line:84%
of multicellular models, both
of them being neural models

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:01.640 align:middle line:84%
kind of building from what we
were thinking about last time.

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:03.860 align:middle line:84%
But now we're going to a
completely different kind

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:09.050 align:middle line:84%
of multicellular model, all the
way up from individual effects

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:12.500 align:middle line:84%
that a single nucleotide-- how
much effect a single nucleotide

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:15.980 align:middle line:84%
can have on a whole organ
system, which in this case

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:17.940 align:middle line:90%
will be cardiovascular.

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:20.270 align:middle line:90%
So we go from--.

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:26.060 align:middle line:84%
And there are a number of
physiome and cardiome projects

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:28.830 align:middle line:90%
that are of active interest.

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:32.440 align:middle line:90%


00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:34.390 align:middle line:84%
Just like the
previous slides that I

00:16:34.390 --> 00:16:37.330 align:middle line:84%
showed showing all the ways
of sharing system models,

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:43.450 align:middle line:84%
many of these efforts are kind
of loose consortium alliances

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:47.890 align:middle line:84%
where people have put together
different models either to deal

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:52.870 align:middle line:84%
with the anatomy,
physiology, or sometimes,

00:16:52.870 --> 00:17:00.580 align:middle line:84%
a integration of molecular to
cellular, cellular to, say,

00:17:00.580 --> 00:17:05.290 align:middle line:84%
neural or muscular, and all
the way up to fluid dynamics

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:08.359 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:17:08.359 --> 00:17:12.310 align:middle line:84%
So let's start with a
single base-pair change.

00:17:12.310 --> 00:17:14.993 align:middle line:84%
And I start with
this one because you

00:17:14.993 --> 00:17:16.910 align:middle line:84%
should feel comfortable
with it at this point.

00:17:16.910 --> 00:17:19.960 align:middle line:90%
We mentioned it briefly before.

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:22.000 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about it
in more detail now.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:24.589 align:middle line:84%
This is the single
nucleotide polymorphism,

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:30.340 align:middle line:84%
which causes the change of the
beta subunit of hemoglobin,

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:37.030 align:middle line:84%
which is a tetramer,
to go from a glutamate,

00:17:37.030 --> 00:17:40.450 align:middle line:84%
normally, in most people at
position six of the beta chain,

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:42.970 align:middle line:90%
to a valine.

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:46.180 align:middle line:84%
So that's the purple
set of tetramers.

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:51.400 align:middle line:84%
Or to tryptophan, and that's
the cyan set on the far right.

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:59.670 align:middle line:84%
And this, from combinations
of X-ray crystallography

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:01.860 align:middle line:84%
and three-dimensional
modeling, and this

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:04.950 align:middle line:84%
is not speculation, just based
on primary sequence data,

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:08.070 align:middle line:84%
even though the primary sequence
here is very, very close,

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:12.400 align:middle line:84%
just a single nucleotide and
a single amino acid change.

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:14.610 align:middle line:84%
Nevertheless, these
authors have been

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:18.840 align:middle line:84%
building this from the
crystallographic data

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:22.200 align:middle line:84%
where not only do you care
about the tetramer itself,

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:24.810 align:middle line:84%
but how the tetramers
interact with one another

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:28.500 align:middle line:84%
to make these long, fibrous
chains, which are considerably

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:31.320 align:middle line:90%
more stable in the sickle cell.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:39.180 align:middle line:84%
And what you see here is sort
of the valine substitution

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:41.760 align:middle line:84%
is locked in one
kind of conformation

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:44.290 align:middle line:90%
and the tryptophan in another.

00:18:44.290 --> 00:18:47.100 align:middle line:84%
And you can see
how these authors

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:51.120 align:middle line:84%
have decided how a
potential fiber can

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:53.280 align:middle line:90%
form in the different cases.

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:57.110 align:middle line:90%


00:18:57.110 --> 00:18:59.870 align:middle line:84%
Now those fibers, in
one way or another,

00:18:59.870 --> 00:19:05.390 align:middle line:84%
which is not totally
mapped out, but you

00:19:05.390 --> 00:19:10.740 align:middle line:84%
affect the efficiency of
the hemoglobin slightly

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:13.940 align:middle line:84%
and the shape of the
cell much more radically.

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:19.200 align:middle line:84%
And this combination
results in the cell,

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:23.090 align:middle line:84%
especially under any kind
of stress, either oxidative

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:25.910 align:middle line:84%
or other metabolic
stress, becoming sickled.

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:29.330 align:middle line:84%
So you can have a combination
of cells which have

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:31.010 align:middle line:90%
varying degrees of sickling.

00:19:31.010 --> 00:19:37.430 align:middle line:84%
Here in this microscopic
slide on the left,

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:40.130 align:middle line:84%
you have both the sickle
cell and the normal cell side

00:19:40.130 --> 00:19:42.210 align:middle line:90%
by side.

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:45.260 align:middle line:84%
I think when we built up the
red blood cell metabolic model,

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:48.050 align:middle line:84%
we talked about some of the
non-metabolic considerations

00:19:48.050 --> 00:19:52.280 align:middle line:84%
of that, which were that its
function is to transport oxygen

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:54.680 align:middle line:90%
and so forth.

00:19:54.680 --> 00:19:58.330 align:middle line:84%
And what we're talking about now
is more a cell membrane issue.

00:19:58.330 --> 00:20:03.680 align:middle line:84%
This is on slide 13, where the
internal environment, which

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:06.050 align:middle line:84%
is the hemoglobin, is
greatly affecting now

00:20:06.050 --> 00:20:09.170 align:middle line:84%
the external environment,
which is the hemodynamic flow

00:20:09.170 --> 00:20:12.320 align:middle line:90%
in the capillaries.

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:15.680 align:middle line:90%


00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:17.840 align:middle line:84%
So this falls under
the heading now

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:21.560 align:middle line:84%
of how is it that we can go from
that single nucleotide change

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:23.720 align:middle line:84%
to a very dramatic
morphological change?

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:25.970 align:middle line:84%
In this case, it's
pathological, but you

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:30.887 align:middle line:84%
could imagine that in the hands
of evolutionary adaptation,

00:20:30.887 --> 00:20:32.720 align:middle line:84%
an organism could take
this and run with it.

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:35.180 align:middle line:84%
Maybe not sickle cell mutation,
but some other one that

00:20:35.180 --> 00:20:38.060 align:middle line:84%
causes some other morphological
change in some other cell

00:20:38.060 --> 00:20:41.120 align:middle line:90%
or complex aggregate of cells.

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:47.750 align:middle line:84%
Because here you can see a whole
variety of different shapes

00:20:47.750 --> 00:20:49.793 align:middle line:90%
of red blood cells.

00:20:49.793 --> 00:20:51.960 align:middle line:84%
And remember, these red
blood cells are very simple.

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:56.900 align:middle line:84%
They have no macromolecular
[INAUDIBLE],, no DNA, no RNA,

00:20:56.900 --> 00:20:59.540 align:middle line:84%
so we're really just talking
about a bag of proteins which

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:03.590 align:middle line:84%
are greatly affected by
these different conditions

00:21:03.590 --> 00:21:05.460 align:middle line:90%
or enzyme deficiencies.

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:07.280 align:middle line:84%
So the system models
that were built up,

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:11.450 align:middle line:84%
where we had the kinetic
parameters for the enzymes,

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:16.100 align:middle line:84%
here can, in principle, be
modeled on the impact it would

00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:21.320 align:middle line:84%
have on the osmolarity, or the
other membrane properties that

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:22.910 align:middle line:84%
were listed in the
previous slide,

00:21:22.910 --> 00:21:25.250 align:middle line:84%
or the sickling that
was in two slides back.

00:21:25.250 --> 00:21:36.680 align:middle line:90%


00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:41.810 align:middle line:84%
So from a single
nucleotide polymorphism,

00:21:41.810 --> 00:21:44.240 align:middle line:84%
we've gone from this
three-dimensional fiber

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:50.000 align:middle line:84%
of hemoglobin to a change in
the three-dimensional structure

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:57.020 align:middle line:84%
of this biconcave
disk to a sickle disk.

00:21:57.020 --> 00:21:59.540 align:middle line:84%
But you can also go,
sort of in parallel,

00:21:59.540 --> 00:22:02.960 align:middle line:84%
the same single-nucleotide
polymorphism, or some like it,

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:05.750 align:middle line:84%
can take you up to
pathogen resistance.

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:09.500 align:middle line:84%
We've already mentioned that
sickle cell hemoglobin can take

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:11.690 align:middle line:90%
you to malarial resistance.

00:22:11.690 --> 00:22:19.010 align:middle line:84%
But in addition, you
can get components

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:22.860 align:middle line:84%
of the enzymatic
metabolic pathway,

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:28.940 align:middle line:84%
such as the one that we
modeled a few lectures back,

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:30.630 align:middle line:90%
such as glutathione peroxidase.

00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:35.150 align:middle line:84%
This is part of the
redox components.

00:22:35.150 --> 00:22:39.110 align:middle line:84%
And here, erythrocytes
that are heterozygous

00:22:39.110 --> 00:22:46.230 align:middle line:84%
for this particular
allele should

00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:48.390 align:middle line:84%
be more efficient in
sheltering the cell membrane

00:22:48.390 --> 00:22:51.090 align:middle line:84%
from irreversible oxidation
in binding hemoglobin caused

00:22:51.090 --> 00:22:53.130 align:middle line:84%
by the oxidant
stress that's exerted

00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:57.660 align:middle line:90%
by the malarial parasite.

00:22:57.660 --> 00:22:59.910 align:middle line:84%
And what they observe
here is actually

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:07.470 align:middle line:84%
an interaction between these
two possible haplotypes.

00:23:07.470 --> 00:23:10.080 align:middle line:84%
So it's actually, you can think
of it as a pair of haplotypes.

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:14.880 align:middle line:84%
It's the one is
the hemoglobin AS,

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:18.000 align:middle line:84%
the one we've been talking
about for sickling,

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:20.880 align:middle line:84%
and then, this
glutathione peroxidase.

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:23.640 align:middle line:84%
And so you can imagine, that
since both of these things

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:27.030 align:middle line:84%
interact with malaria
parasite, that your genotype

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:27.900 align:middle line:90%
will depend--

00:23:27.900 --> 00:23:30.060 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, your phenotype
in respect to malaria

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:33.577 align:middle line:84%
will depend on the alleles
that you have at both cases.

00:23:33.577 --> 00:23:35.910 align:middle line:84%
So you can-- here, you can
have an A over S heterozygote

00:23:35.910 --> 00:23:39.247 align:middle line:90%
and a two over one heterozygote.

00:23:39.247 --> 00:23:41.580 align:middle line:84%
And so that's something to
take into account when you're

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:46.170 align:middle line:84%
trying to do any kind of
predictive modeling or modeling

00:23:46.170 --> 00:23:49.200 align:middle line:84%
to explain the functional
genomics that you

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:51.840 align:middle line:84%
have in a patient who's
a compound heterozygote

00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:53.380 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:23:53.380 --> 00:23:55.590 align:middle line:90%
So now, the third pathway.

00:23:55.590 --> 00:23:57.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, from single-- the same
set of single nucleotide

00:23:57.960 --> 00:23:59.790 align:middle line:84%
polymorphisms that
affect either hemoglobin

00:23:59.790 --> 00:24:02.800 align:middle line:84%
or one of the major enzymes
in the red blood cell.

00:24:02.800 --> 00:24:06.300 align:middle line:90%
Now, on to cell morphology.

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:09.690 align:middle line:90%
On to pathogen interaction.

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:11.670 align:middle line:84%
And finally, to
interaction with drugs.

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:13.290 align:middle line:84%
We talked about
pharmacogenomics.

00:24:13.290 --> 00:24:19.650 align:middle line:84%
Here's another example where you
have the drug-induced oxidative

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:24.330 align:middle line:84%
hemolysis that you occur
with certain enzymopathies

00:24:24.330 --> 00:24:28.470 align:middle line:84%
like glucose-6-phosphate
dehydrogenase.

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:32.550 align:middle line:84%
This enzyme, glucose-6-phosphate
dehydrogenase,

00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:36.660 align:middle line:84%
interacts with drugs
such as primaquine.

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:42.700 align:middle line:84%
And it disrupts mitochondrial
function, heme biosynthesis,

00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:45.700 align:middle line:90%
and so forth, and so on.

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:51.720 align:middle line:84%
This is a very
significant consideration

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:54.420 align:middle line:84%
with a long list
of 20-some drugs

00:24:54.420 --> 00:24:56.460 align:middle line:84%
that has to be taken
to account when

00:24:56.460 --> 00:25:03.490 align:middle line:84%
you have any of a variety of
red blood cell enzyme changes.

00:25:03.490 --> 00:25:08.160 align:middle line:84%
So we've got these three
effects of a limited number

00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:11.310 align:middle line:84%
of single nucleotide
polymorphisms.

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:16.860 align:middle line:84%
How do you make this
transition from--

00:25:16.860 --> 00:25:18.570 align:middle line:84%
and they're all
somewhat interconnected.

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:20.403 align:middle line:84%
Part of the reason it's
resistant to malaria

00:25:20.403 --> 00:25:22.350 align:middle line:84%
is because it's less
effective in its cell

00:25:22.350 --> 00:25:27.750 align:middle line:84%
shape, and its ability to
transport oxygen, and do

00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:29.450 align:middle line:90%
its metabolism.

00:25:29.450 --> 00:25:32.460 align:middle line:84%
And same thing with
drug sensitivity.

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:39.750 align:middle line:84%
How are these changes
in the hemoglobin--

00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:42.690 align:middle line:84%
might they be reflected in
the three-dimensional shape

00:25:42.690 --> 00:25:45.360 align:middle line:84%
of the erythrocyte,
which is a kind

00:25:45.360 --> 00:25:47.940 align:middle line:90%
of a membrane-bound compartment?

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:50.520 align:middle line:84%
And here's a model that
struck me as interesting.

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:56.140 align:middle line:84%
It's not been that
extensively tested since 1998.

00:25:56.140 --> 00:25:58.830 align:middle line:90%
But the idea is that Band3--

00:25:58.830 --> 00:26:01.260 align:middle line:84%
this is one of those
names that just comes out

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:04.320 align:middle line:84%
of the molecular
biologist literature,

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:06.330 align:middle line:84%
that they ran a
gel, and they count

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:08.100 align:middle line:90%
the bands, and number three.

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:13.380 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out it's about 10%
of the red blood cell membrane.

00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:16.650 align:middle line:90%
It's a very abundant protein.

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:20.670 align:middle line:84%
And it's responsible
for the equilibration

00:26:20.670 --> 00:26:24.480 align:middle line:84%
of anions such as carbonate and
chloride across the red blood

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:25.740 align:middle line:90%
cell membrane.

00:26:25.740 --> 00:26:29.160 align:middle line:84%
It's not a pump, it just
kind of allows these anions

00:26:29.160 --> 00:26:31.200 align:middle line:84%
to go across because
the pump here

00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:34.170 align:middle line:84%
is the pumps that are
involved in proton

00:26:34.170 --> 00:26:37.740 align:middle line:84%
and there's also
sodium potassium.

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:40.320 align:middle line:84%
And that's what your ATP in the
red blood cell is going for.

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:42.112 align:middle line:84%
And these are just kind
of following along.

00:26:42.112 --> 00:26:44.460 align:middle line:90%


00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:54.190 align:middle line:84%
The idea here is that if
you change the degree--

00:26:54.190 --> 00:26:57.438 align:middle line:84%
let's see, I think this
is covered in more detail.

00:26:57.438 --> 00:26:58.770 align:middle line:90%
No, sorry.

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:02.405 align:middle line:90%


00:27:02.405 --> 00:27:05.670 align:middle line:84%
The mechanism of
action here is greatly

00:27:05.670 --> 00:27:08.850 align:middle line:84%
affected by its
disulfide effects.

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:13.590 align:middle line:84%
And if you change the redox
components in the red blood

00:27:13.590 --> 00:27:17.400 align:middle line:84%
cell, you get just a slight
change in conformation

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:18.840 align:middle line:90%
of this molecule.

00:27:18.840 --> 00:27:22.140 align:middle line:84%
And then that can result
in a net difference

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:26.040 align:middle line:84%
between the cross-sectional area
of this protein on the outside

00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:29.820 align:middle line:84%
and the inside, which
translates into a net change--

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:34.277 align:middle line:84%
since the phospholipids
don't exchange,

00:27:34.277 --> 00:27:36.360 align:middle line:84%
or the rate constant for
the phospholipid exchange

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:38.760 align:middle line:84%
is slow and known,
then this results

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:45.900 align:middle line:90%
in a conformational change.

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:48.840 align:middle line:90%
Anyway, that's the model.

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:51.900 align:middle line:84%
That connects the
single nucleotide to--

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:54.900 align:middle line:84%
could connect it to the
three-dimensional structure

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:55.770 align:middle line:90%
of the cell.

00:27:55.770 --> 00:27:58.103 align:middle line:84%
Then you want to connect the
three-dimensional structure

00:27:58.103 --> 00:28:01.560 align:middle line:84%
of the cell to its ability
to carry out its function

00:28:01.560 --> 00:28:02.820 align:middle line:90%
in the capillaries.

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:06.390 align:middle line:84%
And its function there
is to allow the fusion

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:12.570 align:middle line:90%
of oxygen and carbon dioxide.

00:28:12.570 --> 00:28:22.520 align:middle line:90%


00:28:22.520 --> 00:28:28.550 align:middle line:84%
So here, each of these cells is
shaped by a mechanical process.

00:28:28.550 --> 00:28:30.350 align:middle line:84%
A mechanical process
here, you can

00:28:30.350 --> 00:28:36.020 align:middle line:84%
take the known or the
measurable mechanical properties

00:28:36.020 --> 00:28:39.230 align:middle line:84%
of a red blood cell
and subject them

00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:45.170 align:middle line:84%
to a method called finite
elements analysis where

00:28:45.170 --> 00:28:49.790 align:middle line:84%
you're solving these partial
differentiable equations.

00:28:49.790 --> 00:28:55.500 align:middle line:84%
And you can calculate the
exchange of the oxygen--

00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:59.270 align:middle line:90%


00:28:59.270 --> 00:29:01.725 align:middle line:84%
alveolar just refers to--
this would be in the lungs.

00:29:01.725 --> 00:29:03.600 align:middle line:84%
This would be a small
capillary in the lungs.

00:29:03.600 --> 00:29:05.017 align:middle line:84%
And you can see
the capillary now,

00:29:05.017 --> 00:29:07.670 align:middle line:84%
the endothelial walls are close
enough so that the red blood

00:29:07.670 --> 00:29:10.828 align:middle line:84%
cells are basically
deformed in their shape

00:29:10.828 --> 00:29:11.870 align:middle line:90%
as they go through there.

00:29:11.870 --> 00:29:14.690 align:middle line:84%
And the exact shapes that
are compatible with this

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:17.240 align:middle line:84%
will determine the rate that
these little arrows that

00:29:17.240 --> 00:29:21.470 align:middle line:84%
go from the oxygen on the
outside the lung epithelium

00:29:21.470 --> 00:29:24.360 align:middle line:90%
get through to the blood cell.

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:31.130 align:middle line:84%
So that's a reference that
deals with that kind of problem.

00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:39.580 align:middle line:84%
Now, as we start thinking about
building up a larger system

00:29:39.580 --> 00:29:42.100 align:middle line:84%
model, the other parts of
the system, each of them--

00:29:42.100 --> 00:29:43.600 align:middle line:84%
so we've got a red
blood cell model,

00:29:43.600 --> 00:29:44.642 align:middle line:90%
it's the metabolic model.

00:29:44.642 --> 00:29:49.000 align:middle line:84%
In principle, it could also be
a shape model and a diffusion

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:50.260 align:middle line:90%
model.

00:29:50.260 --> 00:29:53.360 align:middle line:84%
But the other
parts of the system

00:29:53.360 --> 00:29:58.010 align:middle line:90%
typically involve muscle cells.

00:29:58.010 --> 00:30:07.830 align:middle line:84%
They have the smooth muscles
throughout the entire arterial

00:30:07.830 --> 00:30:12.820 align:middle line:84%
and venous system, and most
significantly, the heart

00:30:12.820 --> 00:30:13.750 align:middle line:90%
muscles.

00:30:13.750 --> 00:30:19.870 align:middle line:84%
And so here you have an example
of action potential in one

00:30:19.870 --> 00:30:23.380 align:middle line:84%
of the ventricular
cells of the dog heart.

00:30:23.380 --> 00:30:27.730 align:middle line:84%
And it includes all the
components that at least we,

00:30:27.730 --> 00:30:34.460 align:middle line:84%
since this is not an entire
course on ventricular heart

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:36.890 align:middle line:90%
modeling.

00:30:36.890 --> 00:30:39.230 align:middle line:84%
This is the sort of
parameters, in terms

00:30:39.230 --> 00:30:42.620 align:middle line:84%
of all the I to the currents for
each of the different potassium

00:30:42.620 --> 00:30:45.980 align:middle line:84%
channels to K, all of--
each of the ones at I

00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:48.680 align:middle line:90%
sub K was all potassium.

00:30:48.680 --> 00:30:52.550 align:middle line:84%
And then there's some in sodium,
and sodium and calcium, calcium

00:30:52.550 --> 00:30:54.607 align:middle line:90%
potassium, sodium alone.

00:30:54.607 --> 00:30:56.690 align:middle line:84%
And so each of these things,
that internal storage

00:30:56.690 --> 00:30:59.900 align:middle line:84%
of calcium [INAUDIBLE] from
one subcellular compartment

00:30:59.900 --> 00:31:03.210 align:middle line:90%
to another and so on.

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:03.920 align:middle line:90%
You get the idea.

00:31:03.920 --> 00:31:07.130 align:middle line:84%
Each of these things has to
have the parameters measured.

00:31:07.130 --> 00:31:10.160 align:middle line:84%
And any of that are
absent, you have

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:16.670 align:middle line:84%
to have reasonable ways
of getting surrogates.

00:31:16.670 --> 00:31:20.330 align:middle line:84%
And so this is just
the sort of data

00:31:20.330 --> 00:31:24.470 align:middle line:84%
that would go into the other
major type of cell that

00:31:24.470 --> 00:31:29.390 align:middle line:84%
comes into this
cardiovascular modeling.

00:31:29.390 --> 00:31:33.260 align:middle line:84%
And finally, you can
integrate this up

00:31:33.260 --> 00:31:36.020 align:middle line:90%
to a fairly complete system.

00:31:36.020 --> 00:31:37.580 align:middle line:84%
It's obviously not
complete until you

00:31:37.580 --> 00:31:40.460 align:middle line:84%
get to the whole organism,
but higher level,

00:31:40.460 --> 00:31:44.260 align:middle line:84%
and how we're talking about
whole body recirculation.

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:47.540 align:middle line:84%
This NSR group, this
is a hypertext link

00:31:47.540 --> 00:31:50.930 align:middle line:84%
for this particular model
which you can download.

00:31:50.930 --> 00:31:52.500 align:middle line:84%
And it has a
four-chambered heart.

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:54.620 align:middle line:84%
We were just talking
about one ventricular

00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:56.300 align:middle line:90%
cell on the previous slide.

00:31:56.300 --> 00:31:58.100 align:middle line:90%
But that would be--

00:31:58.100 --> 00:32:00.980 align:middle line:84%
if we look at the second
box from the top here,

00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:05.130 align:middle line:84%
this is where the heart and
lung model would fit in there.

00:32:05.130 --> 00:32:08.300 align:middle line:84%
It's got seven different organs,
which include kidney, liver,

00:32:08.300 --> 00:32:11.140 align:middle line:84%
lower limbs, and so forth
down at the bottom part

00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:12.990 align:middle line:90%
of this diagram.

00:32:12.990 --> 00:32:14.180 align:middle line:90%
And you get the picture.

00:32:14.180 --> 00:32:23.690 align:middle line:84%
Each of these things, you're
modeling the volumes and flows,

00:32:23.690 --> 00:32:25.790 align:middle line:90%
Now, this is systems biology.

00:32:25.790 --> 00:32:29.540 align:middle line:84%
This is really kind of a
renaissance of interest

00:32:29.540 --> 00:32:32.330 align:middle line:84%
in physiology, which is what it
would have been called before.

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:36.800 align:middle line:84%
And there are some really
interesting mysteries out there

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:41.570 align:middle line:84%
to be solved that are really
only solvable, or possibly only

00:32:41.570 --> 00:32:42.920 align:middle line:90%
solvable, at the system level.

00:32:42.920 --> 00:32:45.140 align:middle line:84%
You never know,
somebody could come up

00:32:45.140 --> 00:32:46.730 align:middle line:84%
and say, oh, yeah,
this is really

00:32:46.730 --> 00:32:50.340 align:middle line:84%
well understood immunology
and that answers everything.

00:32:50.340 --> 00:32:55.307 align:middle line:84%
But even if it's some
kind of immunology,

00:32:55.307 --> 00:32:57.140 align:middle line:84%
you still have to say,
how does it play out?

00:32:57.140 --> 00:32:59.473 align:middle line:84%
And what happens is we've
been talking about sickle cell

00:32:59.473 --> 00:33:02.960 align:middle line:84%
disease, and you can
have fairly mild pain

00:33:02.960 --> 00:33:05.130 align:middle line:90%
as one of the major symptoms.

00:33:05.130 --> 00:33:06.980 align:middle line:84%
But then all of a
sudden, out of nowhere,

00:33:06.980 --> 00:33:09.470 align:middle line:84%
you'll get this
multi-organ system failure

00:33:09.470 --> 00:33:13.850 align:middle line:84%
where almost every major
organ in that previous slide,

00:33:13.850 --> 00:33:15.590 align:middle line:84%
lungs and so forth,
fill up with fluid

00:33:15.590 --> 00:33:19.082 align:middle line:84%
and you have a very
high chance of dying.

00:33:19.082 --> 00:33:21.290 align:middle line:84%
And this could happen to
every one of us in this room

00:33:21.290 --> 00:33:23.840 align:middle line:84%
because it's not
restricted to sickle cell.

00:33:23.840 --> 00:33:30.980 align:middle line:84%
If any of you have severe
burn, or car crash, major bone

00:33:30.980 --> 00:33:33.590 align:middle line:84%
injuries, and so forth,
you have a very good chance

00:33:33.590 --> 00:33:36.030 align:middle line:84%
of getting into
multi-organ system failure.

00:33:36.030 --> 00:33:39.500 align:middle line:84%
And it really isn't
known how that plays out.

00:33:39.500 --> 00:33:42.290 align:middle line:84%
I mean, not even that-- not
just a quantitative model,

00:33:42.290 --> 00:33:45.020 align:middle line:90%
but even qualitatively.

00:33:45.020 --> 00:33:49.220 align:middle line:84%
So I think this is something--
there are projects now

00:33:49.220 --> 00:33:54.620 align:middle line:84%
to get genomic data collection,
both genetic and expression

00:33:54.620 --> 00:34:00.470 align:middle line:84%
data, and time will tell whether
that is actually the best

00:34:00.470 --> 00:34:02.770 align:middle line:90%
route to solving that mystery.

00:34:02.770 --> 00:34:04.340 align:middle line:90%
So that was multi-organ.

00:34:04.340 --> 00:34:07.430 align:middle line:84%
Now we're talking
about multi-organism.

00:34:07.430 --> 00:34:11.690 align:middle line:84%
After you've built up
an entire organism,

00:34:11.690 --> 00:34:13.670 align:middle line:84%
then you want to
know how it acts

00:34:13.670 --> 00:34:18.560 align:middle line:84%
at the next level of network
analysis, which is how does it

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:22.489 align:middle line:90%
fit in with other organisms?

00:34:22.489 --> 00:34:26.330 align:middle line:84%
None of us, almost
no organism, really

00:34:26.330 --> 00:34:29.690 align:middle line:84%
belongs in ecological
niche all by itself.

00:34:29.690 --> 00:34:35.659 align:middle line:84%
And some of the modeling we
are taught in this course

00:34:35.659 --> 00:34:37.730 align:middle line:90%
could be called simulation.

00:34:37.730 --> 00:34:43.820 align:middle line:84%
And probably one of the
longest-lasting computer

00:34:43.820 --> 00:34:48.440 align:middle line:84%
games in the world, and
one of the most successful,

00:34:48.440 --> 00:34:52.550 align:middle line:90%
I've heard, is The Sims.

00:34:52.550 --> 00:34:54.530 align:middle line:90%
It started with SimCity in--

00:34:54.530 --> 00:34:57.440 align:middle line:90%


00:34:57.440 --> 00:34:58.520 align:middle line:90%
SimCity in '87.

00:34:58.520 --> 00:35:00.995 align:middle line:90%


00:35:00.995 --> 00:35:02.870 align:middle line:84%
And then this particular
one illustrated here

00:35:02.870 --> 00:35:06.470 align:middle line:84%
is SimLife, which is all
about ecological modeling.

00:35:06.470 --> 00:35:08.870 align:middle line:84%
And it's not entirely
different from what

00:35:08.870 --> 00:35:12.530 align:middle line:84%
a serious ecological-modeling
program would do.

00:35:12.530 --> 00:35:14.870 align:middle line:84%
It certainly has a lot of
the interesting parameters

00:35:14.870 --> 00:35:18.410 align:middle line:90%
such as the lifespan here.

00:35:18.410 --> 00:35:21.890 align:middle line:84%
You're doing demographics
here so you have the lifespan,

00:35:21.890 --> 00:35:28.850 align:middle line:84%
the amount of food needed,
the size, kind of vision,

00:35:28.850 --> 00:35:32.300 align:middle line:84%
roaming, so forth, of all these
different kinds of animals.

00:35:32.300 --> 00:35:35.090 align:middle line:90%


00:35:35.090 --> 00:35:38.960 align:middle line:84%
You can have plants, you
have herbivores, carnivores,

00:35:38.960 --> 00:35:39.930 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:35:39.930 --> 00:35:42.560 align:middle line:84%
And then you can set up
the population sizes,

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:45.980 align:middle line:84%
and then it will do
simulations based

00:35:45.980 --> 00:35:51.440 align:middle line:84%
on that, where each individual
animal is tracked in terms

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:53.570 align:middle line:90%
of its position and quantity.

00:35:53.570 --> 00:35:59.360 align:middle line:84%
And as you would expect,
as the carnivores build up,

00:35:59.360 --> 00:36:00.860 align:middle line:84%
then they knock
down the herbivores,

00:36:00.860 --> 00:36:04.160 align:middle line:84%
and the plants go
up, and so forth.

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:06.680 align:middle line:84%
And it goes through
these cycles.

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:08.990 align:middle line:84%
And so this is basically
a stochastic model,

00:36:08.990 --> 00:36:13.730 align:middle line:84%
just like the stochastic models
that we had for molecules,

00:36:13.730 --> 00:36:16.010 align:middle line:90%
but here, at the organism level.

00:36:16.010 --> 00:36:19.160 align:middle line:84%
And what's happening here in
the slower right-hand section

00:36:19.160 --> 00:36:22.580 align:middle line:84%
is you've got little
green plants getting

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:25.160 align:middle line:84%
eaten by herbivores, and
the herbivores getting

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:26.360 align:middle line:90%
eaten by carnivores.

00:36:26.360 --> 00:36:29.050 align:middle line:90%


00:36:29.050 --> 00:36:35.210 align:middle line:84%
Now, hopefully, this
course has already

00:36:35.210 --> 00:36:40.100 align:middle line:84%
or will inspire you to
really think globally.

00:36:40.100 --> 00:36:42.500 align:middle line:84%
To think not only
globally in terms

00:36:42.500 --> 00:36:48.020 align:middle line:84%
of how little pieces
of molecular tools

00:36:48.020 --> 00:36:50.750 align:middle line:84%
fit together into
systems and networks,

00:36:50.750 --> 00:36:53.600 align:middle line:84%
but how our systems
and networks we model

00:36:53.600 --> 00:36:58.430 align:middle line:84%
fit into the big picture of what
are really important problems.

00:36:58.430 --> 00:37:04.550 align:middle line:84%
Maybe a slightly
improved Viagra is not

00:37:04.550 --> 00:37:08.330 align:middle line:84%
in the same category as
a new tuberculosis drug

00:37:08.330 --> 00:37:09.080 align:middle line:90%
or maybe it is.

00:37:09.080 --> 00:37:13.130 align:middle line:84%
I mean, you decide for yourself
what is thinking globally.

00:37:13.130 --> 00:37:14.870 align:middle line:84%
But you have to act
locally, and that's

00:37:14.870 --> 00:37:16.162 align:middle line:90%
what we're doing in this class.

00:37:16.162 --> 00:37:19.070 align:middle line:90%


00:37:19.070 --> 00:37:20.780 align:middle line:84%
When we're thinking
globally, and we're

00:37:20.780 --> 00:37:23.240 align:middle line:84%
thinking about
ecological systems,

00:37:23.240 --> 00:37:28.490 align:middle line:84%
we're thinking about the
lithosphere, for the most part,

00:37:28.490 --> 00:37:32.150 align:middle line:84%
and its interaction
with the hydrosphere.

00:37:32.150 --> 00:37:40.030 align:middle line:84%
The lithosphere is
mostly silicon dioxide,

00:37:40.030 --> 00:37:44.880 align:middle line:84%
a tiny bit of carbon, and it
gets very hot, very quickly.

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:52.760 align:middle line:84%
So only the top 0.1%, that's
the top four kilometers of it,

00:37:52.760 --> 00:37:56.510 align:middle line:84%
is survivable in any type
of organism we know of.

00:37:56.510 --> 00:37:59.720 align:middle line:84%
About 110 degrees centigrade
is where organisms start

00:37:59.720 --> 00:38:01.940 align:middle line:90%
having a great deal of trouble.

00:38:01.940 --> 00:38:03.740 align:middle line:84%
You and I would have
trouble before that.

00:38:03.740 --> 00:38:07.570 align:middle line:90%


00:38:07.570 --> 00:38:11.010 align:middle line:84%
The biosphere here
is about 3 times 10

00:38:11.010 --> 00:38:21.150 align:middle line:84%
to the 15th grams, my
estimate for marine organisms.

00:38:21.150 --> 00:38:27.210 align:middle line:84%
And maybe 10 to the 18th grams
for all land plants and animals

00:38:27.210 --> 00:38:30.150 align:middle line:90%
and microorganisms.

00:38:30.150 --> 00:38:36.425 align:middle line:84%
The microbial hydrosphere
here is about 10

00:38:36.425 --> 00:38:41.580 align:middle line:84%
to the 21st milliliters,
which works out about 10

00:38:41.580 --> 00:38:42.960 align:middle line:90%
to the 27th cells.

00:38:42.960 --> 00:38:44.930 align:middle line:84%
A phenomenal number of
these cells, about 10

00:38:44.930 --> 00:38:47.640 align:middle line:84%
to the 26th of those
cells is a single species,

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:52.710 align:middle line:84%
which is prochlorococcus,
which is responsible for maybe

00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:56.670 align:middle line:84%
about 50% of the
Earth's photosynthesis.

00:38:56.670 --> 00:39:00.660 align:middle line:84%
A lot of that photosynthesis
does not end up in fixed carbon

00:39:00.660 --> 00:39:03.570 align:middle line:84%
because it is
immediately consumed

00:39:03.570 --> 00:39:11.070 align:middle line:84%
by one of its other
predators, basically.

00:39:11.070 --> 00:39:18.097 align:middle line:84%
But there's really quite a
lot of cells out there which,

00:39:18.097 --> 00:39:19.680 align:middle line:84%
to the extent that
they're well-mixed,

00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:21.930 align:middle line:84%
and they're not-- of course,
it's not perfectly mixed,

00:39:21.930 --> 00:39:23.770 align:middle line:84%
all the Pacific and
Atlantic and so forth.

00:39:23.770 --> 00:39:25.860 align:middle line:84%
But it's a considerably
more well-mixed,

00:39:25.860 --> 00:39:27.480 align:middle line:84%
say, than the
lithosphere organisms,

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:31.390 align:middle line:84%
where you have organisms down
a kilometer into the ground.

00:39:31.390 --> 00:39:34.900 align:middle line:84%
They don't move
around very rapidly.

00:39:34.900 --> 00:39:36.650 align:middle line:84%
So when you have a
population of that size

00:39:36.650 --> 00:39:38.610 align:middle line:84%
as you remember from one
of our first lectures

00:39:38.610 --> 00:39:41.520 align:middle line:84%
on population size, the
effect of population size

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:49.578 align:middle line:84%
determines the rate of drift and
the optimality of the organism.

00:39:49.578 --> 00:39:51.120 align:middle line:84%
So one of the things
that we do, when

00:39:51.120 --> 00:39:54.660 align:middle line:84%
we've been talking about
mining the biosphere, one

00:39:54.660 --> 00:39:56.160 align:middle line:84%
of the things that
we're looking for

00:39:56.160 --> 00:40:03.540 align:middle line:84%
are new tools that we can
use for nanoengineering.

00:40:03.540 --> 00:40:07.170 align:middle line:84%
And kind of one of my pet ones
that I'm becoming interested

00:40:07.170 --> 00:40:08.670 align:middle line:84%
in, we don't work
on the same thing,

00:40:08.670 --> 00:40:12.870 align:middle line:84%
but it is kind of interesting,
is a set of-- we mentioned this

00:40:12.870 --> 00:40:18.715 align:middle line:84%
briefly in the drug protein
interaction lecture where--

00:40:18.715 --> 00:40:21.090 align:middle line:84%
but I don't think I mentioned
this particular one-- where

00:40:21.090 --> 00:40:24.300 align:middle line:84%
you have polyketides
that go together.

00:40:24.300 --> 00:40:28.050 align:middle line:84%
It's another polymer that
has certain similarities

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:29.670 align:middle line:84%
to the basic polymers
we talked about,

00:40:29.670 --> 00:40:33.990 align:middle line:84%
but each step in it had
to have a protein enzyme--

00:40:33.990 --> 00:40:36.550 align:middle line:84%
an enzymatic domain
to accomplish that.

00:40:36.550 --> 00:40:38.550 align:middle line:84%
And so one of these
is tetracycline.

00:40:38.550 --> 00:40:41.040 align:middle line:84%
And this is one of
the more aromatic kind

00:40:41.040 --> 00:40:42.900 align:middle line:84%
of coupled-aromatic
compounds that's

00:40:42.900 --> 00:40:44.940 align:middle line:84%
made because you have
these [INAUDIBLE]

00:40:44.940 --> 00:40:50.250 align:middle line:84%
and aromatases in addition
to the polymerization steps.

00:40:50.250 --> 00:40:52.350 align:middle line:84%
And so you make this
thing that looks

00:40:52.350 --> 00:40:55.950 align:middle line:84%
kind of like a poly
aromatic hydrocarbon.

00:40:55.950 --> 00:41:01.200 align:middle line:84%
And also in nature, you will
find, in soot, in forest fires,

00:41:01.200 --> 00:41:02.970 align:middle line:84%
in all kinds of
natural phenomena,

00:41:02.970 --> 00:41:07.410 align:middle line:84%
you will find polycyclic
aromatic hydrocarbons, which

00:41:07.410 --> 00:41:10.710 align:middle line:84%
some of you may know about
as the potent carcinogens,

00:41:10.710 --> 00:41:14.670 align:middle line:84%
but they're also just
natural components.

00:41:14.670 --> 00:41:17.040 align:middle line:84%
But they also-- you
can start to see this

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:19.650 align:middle line:84%
looks a little bit like the
buckyballs and buckytubes

00:41:19.650 --> 00:41:21.025 align:middle line:84%
that we talked
about when we were

00:41:21.025 --> 00:41:24.570 align:middle line:84%
talking about
molecular-type transistors.

00:41:24.570 --> 00:41:29.790 align:middle line:84%
So the possibility of mining
the biosphere for enzymes

00:41:29.790 --> 00:41:31.950 align:middle line:84%
that act to
synthesize or degrade

00:41:31.950 --> 00:41:33.510 align:middle line:84%
this class of
compounds, I think,

00:41:33.510 --> 00:41:34.930 align:middle line:90%
would be just an example.

00:41:34.930 --> 00:41:36.130 align:middle line:90%
We could list many others.

00:41:36.130 --> 00:41:39.150 align:middle line:84%
But the part of it is
just to dream, to imagine,

00:41:39.150 --> 00:41:41.590 align:middle line:84%
what it is would like
to find out there.

00:41:41.590 --> 00:41:46.950 align:middle line:84%
And if there's an
abundant source of it,

00:41:46.950 --> 00:41:48.720 align:middle line:90%
then you should be able to--

00:41:48.720 --> 00:41:51.010 align:middle line:84%
abundant source of, say,
the compound, in this case,

00:41:51.010 --> 00:41:55.030 align:middle line:84%
you need to be able to find
a microorganism and an enzyme

00:41:55.030 --> 00:41:57.060 align:middle line:84%
that goes with that
because there's a truly

00:41:57.060 --> 00:41:59.190 align:middle line:90%
phenomenal amount of diversity.

00:41:59.190 --> 00:42:02.370 align:middle line:90%


00:42:02.370 --> 00:42:05.580 align:middle line:84%
Another very important
global consideration,

00:42:05.580 --> 00:42:08.610 align:middle line:84%
rather than just mining
it for new tools,

00:42:08.610 --> 00:42:11.520 align:middle line:84%
is thinking about ways that
either we could engineer

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:16.890 align:middle line:84%
or accidentally mess
up our entire planet,

00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:22.800 align:middle line:84%
as we could be doing with
global warming or could do.

00:42:22.800 --> 00:42:25.860 align:middle line:84%
And perhaps it's naive
to think that we actually

00:42:25.860 --> 00:42:27.690 align:middle line:84%
are having such a
big effect because we

00:42:27.690 --> 00:42:32.100 align:middle line:84%
know that global warming changes
periodically over millennia.

00:42:32.100 --> 00:42:35.110 align:middle line:90%


00:42:35.110 --> 00:42:40.030 align:middle line:84%
But it is very clear
from the record

00:42:40.030 --> 00:42:42.410 align:middle line:84%
just exactly how much carbon
dioxide we are releasing

00:42:42.410 --> 00:42:46.258 align:middle line:84%
and it makes sense, that it
is consistent with the kind

00:42:46.258 --> 00:42:47.800 align:middle line:84%
of temperature
changes that have been

00:42:47.800 --> 00:42:50.530 align:middle line:84%
observed since the
Industrial Revolution.

00:42:50.530 --> 00:42:55.790 align:middle line:84%
In any case, when you
look at, in particular,

00:42:55.790 --> 00:42:57.850 align:middle line:90%
the Southern Ocean--

00:42:57.850 --> 00:42:59.958 align:middle line:90%
up at the top of slide 27--

00:42:59.958 --> 00:43:02.000 align:middle line:84%
you can see there is a
few places, in particular,

00:43:02.000 --> 00:43:04.330 align:middle line:84%
Southern Ocean seems like
it's a prime candidate where

00:43:04.330 --> 00:43:07.510 align:middle line:84%
you have high nutrients
but low chlorophyll.

00:43:07.510 --> 00:43:09.730 align:middle line:84%
And you say, well, why would
you have high nutrients

00:43:09.730 --> 00:43:14.930 align:middle line:84%
but very little of the
chlorophyll around,

00:43:14.930 --> 00:43:20.170 align:middle line:84%
which is a tipoff that you have
the photosynthetic bacteria.

00:43:20.170 --> 00:43:23.500 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is that
you have a limitation

00:43:23.500 --> 00:43:24.670 align:middle line:90%
of some micronutrient.

00:43:24.670 --> 00:43:29.350 align:middle line:84%
Micronutrient means it's not
needed in the vast quantities

00:43:29.350 --> 00:43:32.270 align:middle line:84%
that you need nitrogen,
phosphorus, carbon, oxygen,

00:43:32.270 --> 00:43:33.470 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:43:33.470 --> 00:43:36.070 align:middle line:84%
So iron, typically, is
the limiting micronutrient

00:43:36.070 --> 00:43:37.330 align:middle line:90%
in the Southern Ocean.

00:43:37.330 --> 00:43:40.810 align:middle line:84%
And so there actually have
been little pilot experiments

00:43:40.810 --> 00:43:44.890 align:middle line:84%
to drop iron off the
backs of huge tankers.

00:43:44.890 --> 00:43:47.260 align:middle line:84%
And there are now at
least seven patents

00:43:47.260 --> 00:43:49.720 align:middle line:84%
that have been filed
on doing this in order

00:43:49.720 --> 00:43:53.740 align:middle line:84%
to balance out the carbon
credits for different nations.

00:43:53.740 --> 00:43:57.700 align:middle line:84%
This is potentially a very big
bit of terrestrial engineering

00:43:57.700 --> 00:43:58.840 align:middle line:90%
that might happen.

00:43:58.840 --> 00:44:04.240 align:middle line:84%
It involves trying to
change phytoplankton.

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:08.450 align:middle line:84%
And as this line here
begins to point out,

00:44:08.450 --> 00:44:11.050 align:middle line:84%
even though
phytoplankton are only 1%

00:44:11.050 --> 00:44:13.750 align:middle line:84%
of the total global
biomass, it's about 50%

00:44:13.750 --> 00:44:16.940 align:middle line:90%
of the carbon fixation.

00:44:16.940 --> 00:44:19.570 align:middle line:84%
What is the source of
this 50-fold anomaly?

00:44:19.570 --> 00:44:24.530 align:middle line:84%
Why is it that even
though it's doing--

00:44:24.530 --> 00:44:26.410 align:middle line:84%
how is it doing 50% of
the carbon fixation?

00:44:26.410 --> 00:44:28.750 align:middle line:84%
And what's happening
is a lot of the carbon

00:44:28.750 --> 00:44:32.443 align:middle line:84%
is going right back
out after being fixed.

00:44:32.443 --> 00:44:34.360 align:middle line:84%
Instead of settling to
the bottom of the ocean

00:44:34.360 --> 00:44:40.030 align:middle line:84%
and never bothering us
again, it gets returned.

00:44:40.030 --> 00:44:42.280 align:middle line:84%
And the exact modeling
of that requires

00:44:42.280 --> 00:44:45.460 align:middle line:84%
a much deeper knowledge
of exactly what organisms

00:44:45.460 --> 00:44:46.810 align:middle line:90%
are present in the ocean.

00:44:46.810 --> 00:44:48.590 align:middle line:84%
One of the problems
with this however,

00:44:48.590 --> 00:44:52.750 align:middle line:84%
is a genomic problem, in
a certain sense, which

00:44:52.750 --> 00:44:56.680 align:middle line:84%
is that very few
of these organisms

00:44:56.680 --> 00:44:58.840 align:middle line:84%
can be cultivated
in the laboratory.

00:44:58.840 --> 00:45:02.230 align:middle line:84%
On the order of 99.9% of the
organisms that you sample

00:45:02.230 --> 00:45:04.180 align:middle line:84%
from the ocean,
or from the soil,

00:45:04.180 --> 00:45:07.090 align:middle line:84%
a number of different
environments,

00:45:07.090 --> 00:45:09.350 align:middle line:84%
do not grow well
in the laboratory.

00:45:09.350 --> 00:45:13.120 align:middle line:84%
So if you were to
study them, some people

00:45:13.120 --> 00:45:15.880 align:middle line:84%
are feeling that the best
route to studying them

00:45:15.880 --> 00:45:19.030 align:middle line:90%
is going directly for--

00:45:19.030 --> 00:45:21.100 align:middle line:84%
looking at their genomes,
without necessarily

00:45:21.100 --> 00:45:23.632 align:middle line:84%
being able to grow
them in the laboratory.

00:45:23.632 --> 00:45:26.410 align:middle line:90%


00:45:26.410 --> 00:45:30.850 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we talk
about this problem of--

00:45:30.850 --> 00:45:34.780 align:middle line:84%
even if we fertilize
the oceans, and we

00:45:34.780 --> 00:45:37.240 align:middle line:84%
can model this whole procedure,
I won't go into details.

00:45:37.240 --> 00:45:47.390 align:middle line:84%
But if there are predators
there that take the fixed carbon

00:45:47.390 --> 00:45:49.820 align:middle line:84%
and turn it back
into carbon dioxide,

00:45:49.820 --> 00:45:52.190 align:middle line:84%
you want to be able
to monitor that.

00:45:52.190 --> 00:45:55.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, of course, the ocean
is a large, complex set

00:45:55.670 --> 00:45:59.540 align:middle line:84%
of prokaryotic
autotrophs that are, say,

00:45:59.540 --> 00:46:02.450 align:middle line:84%
photosynthetic bacteria, and
eukaryotic photosynthetic

00:46:02.450 --> 00:46:08.210 align:middle line:84%
bacteria, and prokaryotic
and eukaryotic predators

00:46:08.210 --> 00:46:09.570 align:middle line:90%
of various sorts.

00:46:09.570 --> 00:46:12.470 align:middle line:84%
And this is, I think, one
of the more interesting

00:46:12.470 --> 00:46:18.560 align:middle line:84%
predator-prey differential
equation models.

00:46:18.560 --> 00:46:21.380 align:middle line:84%
It's almost the same as many
of the other differential

00:46:21.380 --> 00:46:26.880 align:middle line:84%
equations that we did from
the first lecture on growth.

00:46:26.880 --> 00:46:29.180 align:middle line:84%
We did the logistic
equation, some

00:46:29.180 --> 00:46:32.018 align:middle line:84%
of the ones we did on
repressor function, and so on.

00:46:32.018 --> 00:46:34.310 align:middle line:84%
But the thing that's kind of
interesting about this one

00:46:34.310 --> 00:46:40.160 align:middle line:84%
is it's not simply a
single-cell bacteria

00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:43.310 align:middle line:84%
being eaten by a
single-celled heterotroph,

00:46:43.310 --> 00:46:45.020 align:middle line:84%
let's say, a blue-green
algae being eaten

00:46:45.020 --> 00:46:46.490 align:middle line:90%
by a single-cell heterotroph.

00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:48.940 align:middle line:84%
Here, they are
both multicellular.

00:46:48.940 --> 00:46:50.690 align:middle line:84%
They're sort of the
minimal multicellular.

00:46:50.690 --> 00:46:53.240 align:middle line:84%
But because they
are-- so Chlorella

00:46:53.240 --> 00:46:56.030 align:middle line:84%
is one of the smallest
plants, multicellular plants.

00:46:56.030 --> 00:47:03.920 align:middle line:84%
And Brachionus is a small
rotifer, a multicellular

00:47:03.920 --> 00:47:04.515 align:middle line:90%
animal.

00:47:04.515 --> 00:47:06.140 align:middle line:84%
Because they're
multi-cellular, though,

00:47:06.140 --> 00:47:11.690 align:middle line:84%
now you have the demographic
mortality and fecundity

00:47:11.690 --> 00:47:14.120 align:middle line:84%
of each of these things
that has to be modeled in.

00:47:14.120 --> 00:47:18.950 align:middle line:84%
That's the m and the
lambda for Brachionus.

00:47:18.950 --> 00:47:25.400 align:middle line:84%
And so this plays into these
equations where you have here,

00:47:25.400 --> 00:47:28.700 align:middle line:84%
in the upper-right
of slide 28, the rate

00:47:28.700 --> 00:47:34.130 align:middle line:84%
of change of nitrogen
with respect to time,

00:47:34.130 --> 00:47:36.950 align:middle line:90%
and concentration of Chlorella.

00:47:36.950 --> 00:47:40.370 align:middle line:84%
So nitrogen is N,
Chlorella is C.

00:47:40.370 --> 00:47:47.960 align:middle line:84%
The R is the Brachionus
that are reproducing

00:47:47.960 --> 00:47:51.390 align:middle line:84%
and B are the Brachionus
which are the total.

00:47:51.390 --> 00:47:54.410 align:middle line:84%
And you can see each of these
equations and how it plays out.

00:47:54.410 --> 00:47:55.940 align:middle line:90%
Now this is how--

00:47:55.940 --> 00:47:58.640 align:middle line:84%
and the way they play
it out here is you do it

00:47:58.640 --> 00:48:01.460 align:middle line:90%
by dilution rate, delta, here.

00:48:01.460 --> 00:48:12.950 align:middle line:84%
And the dilution rate is shown
along the horizontal x-axis

00:48:12.950 --> 00:48:14.990 align:middle line:84%
for each of these
three plots here.

00:48:14.990 --> 00:48:19.430 align:middle line:84%
And what they're modeling
is on the far left,

00:48:19.430 --> 00:48:26.790 align:middle line:84%
the nitrogen concentration as
a function of dilution rate.

00:48:26.790 --> 00:48:34.640 align:middle line:84%
And you can see you get these
different sectors of behavior.

00:48:34.640 --> 00:48:36.380 align:middle line:84%
When you look at
the Chlorella, this

00:48:36.380 --> 00:48:40.970 align:middle line:84%
is a green, photosynthetic,
multicellular organism,

00:48:40.970 --> 00:48:43.760 align:middle line:84%
in units of millions of
cells per milliliter,

00:48:43.760 --> 00:48:47.620 align:middle line:90%
that can take different--

00:48:47.620 --> 00:48:51.770 align:middle line:84%
it take different
pathways here where,

00:48:51.770 --> 00:48:53.420 align:middle line:84%
as a function of
dilution rate, you

00:48:53.420 --> 00:48:55.610 align:middle line:84%
get this bifurcation
where you get

00:48:55.610 --> 00:49:03.380 align:middle line:84%
a huge split in the possible
concentrations of Chlorella

00:49:03.380 --> 00:49:07.220 align:middle line:84%
is the one curve and the
Brachionus, the predator,

00:49:07.220 --> 00:49:08.315 align:middle line:90%
is the other curve.

00:49:08.315 --> 00:49:10.190 align:middle line:84%
This kind of bifurcation
is the sort of thing

00:49:10.190 --> 00:49:14.810 align:middle line:84%
we talked about earlier,
both in the logistic equation

00:49:14.810 --> 00:49:24.910 align:middle line:84%
and in the
self-exclusion modeling.

00:49:24.910 --> 00:49:29.290 align:middle line:84%
And in the upper-right-hand,
here's our friend

00:49:29.290 --> 00:49:31.320 align:middle line:84%
the coefficient of
variation in percent,

00:49:31.320 --> 00:49:36.160 align:middle line:84%
ranging from 0 to 80 or
so on the vertical axis

00:49:36.160 --> 00:49:37.730 align:middle line:90%
and the dilution rate.

00:49:37.730 --> 00:49:42.550 align:middle line:84%
And again, you can see that you
get a peak in the dilution rate

00:49:42.550 --> 00:49:44.470 align:middle line:90%
at around 0.75.

00:49:44.470 --> 00:49:49.550 align:middle line:84%
The bottom two are models, and
the upper right is the data.

00:49:49.550 --> 00:49:51.880 align:middle line:84%
You can actually see, when
you run real Chlorella

00:49:51.880 --> 00:49:55.150 align:middle line:84%
and Brachionus in here, that you
do get a peak just as you would

00:49:55.150 --> 00:49:57.295 align:middle line:84%
predict in this kind
of bifurcation analysis

00:49:57.295 --> 00:49:59.680 align:middle line:90%
in kinetic modeling.

00:49:59.680 --> 00:50:04.960 align:middle line:84%
That's due to-- where you
get interesting behavior

00:50:04.960 --> 00:50:09.040 align:middle line:84%
in this complicated-- or this
fairly simple model ecosystem

00:50:09.040 --> 00:50:10.960 align:middle line:90%
where you just have two species.

00:50:10.960 --> 00:50:15.070 align:middle line:84%
In the ocean, of course,
you have a lot more.

00:50:15.070 --> 00:50:17.230 align:middle line:84%
Now, that's out
in the wide world

00:50:17.230 --> 00:50:20.890 align:middle line:84%
where you have 10
to the 26 cells.

00:50:20.890 --> 00:50:24.310 align:middle line:84%
But inside every one of us
in this room, no offense,

00:50:24.310 --> 00:50:29.170 align:middle line:84%
but there are about 10 times
as many non-human cells

00:50:29.170 --> 00:50:32.050 align:middle line:84%
in each of us as
are human cells.

00:50:32.050 --> 00:50:35.020 align:middle line:90%
And we have our own ecology.

00:50:35.020 --> 00:50:39.970 align:middle line:84%
And there is a literature
where certain organisms

00:50:39.970 --> 00:50:41.740 align:middle line:84%
we know cause
diseases, and they're

00:50:41.740 --> 00:50:44.980 align:middle line:84%
put into one category,
infectious diseases,

00:50:44.980 --> 00:50:46.450 align:middle line:84%
but then there's
another set that

00:50:46.450 --> 00:50:48.140 align:middle line:90%
takes some time to sort out.

00:50:48.140 --> 00:50:50.110 align:middle line:84%
And you can see here
are candidate diseases

00:50:50.110 --> 00:50:51.520 align:middle line:90%
and candidate organisms.

00:50:51.520 --> 00:50:53.980 align:middle line:84%
And all these little bars
that are going in here

00:50:53.980 --> 00:50:57.310 align:middle line:84%
are references that
you can look up

00:50:57.310 --> 00:51:00.520 align:middle line:84%
where maybe it's not
an airtight case yet,

00:51:00.520 --> 00:51:02.320 align:middle line:84%
or maybe it's not
even-- maybe there's

00:51:02.320 --> 00:51:05.110 align:middle line:84%
controversial or
discredited [INAUDIBLE],,

00:51:05.110 --> 00:51:07.990 align:middle line:84%
but these are links where,
eventually, this will be

00:51:07.990 --> 00:51:10.000 align:middle line:90%
called an infectious disease.

00:51:10.000 --> 00:51:12.520 align:middle line:84%
Helicobacter pylori
causes stomach ulcers.

00:51:12.520 --> 00:51:14.935 align:middle line:84%
I think that fact's
basically already accepted.

00:51:14.935 --> 00:51:17.980 align:middle line:90%


00:51:17.980 --> 00:51:20.260 align:middle line:84%
Some Australian
doctor named Marshall

00:51:20.260 --> 00:51:22.093 align:middle line:84%
decided to drink a
bunch of Helicobacter

00:51:22.093 --> 00:51:24.010 align:middle line:84%
to prove to his colleagues,
who didn't believe

00:51:24.010 --> 00:51:26.830 align:middle line:84%
any stomach ulcers were caused
by Helicobacter, much less all

00:51:26.830 --> 00:51:27.640 align:middle line:90%
of them.

00:51:27.640 --> 00:51:31.960 align:middle line:84%
And he drank it, he cured
himself, he drank it again.

00:51:31.960 --> 00:51:37.420 align:middle line:84%
And he caused stomach
ulcers in himself.

00:51:37.420 --> 00:51:41.560 align:middle line:84%
And hopefully, none
of you will volunteer

00:51:41.560 --> 00:51:45.190 align:middle line:84%
to do similar things with
some of these other nasty guys

00:51:45.190 --> 00:51:47.890 align:middle line:84%
here because I think some
of the list of things

00:51:47.890 --> 00:51:50.320 align:middle line:84%
that they cause are a little
more serious than stomach

00:51:50.320 --> 00:51:52.990 align:middle line:84%
ulcers, and the cures
are a little less obvious

00:51:52.990 --> 00:51:55.240 align:middle line:84%
than the cures for a
gram-negative bacterium

00:51:55.240 --> 00:51:58.840 align:middle line:90%
like Helicobacter.

00:51:58.840 --> 00:52:01.600 align:middle line:84%
There have been
various efforts started

00:52:01.600 --> 00:52:05.950 align:middle line:84%
to actually mine what's the
connection with genomics

00:52:05.950 --> 00:52:07.210 align:middle line:90%
and computational biology?

00:52:07.210 --> 00:52:12.340 align:middle line:84%
To mine the transcriptome
for evidence of these.

00:52:12.340 --> 00:52:14.140 align:middle line:84%
You can look for
bacteria very easily

00:52:14.140 --> 00:52:19.660 align:middle line:84%
because they have ribosomal RNA
components which you can PCR.

00:52:19.660 --> 00:52:25.600 align:middle line:84%
Viruses are more complicated
because they're not conserved,

00:52:25.600 --> 00:52:28.390 align:middle line:84%
or they don't have any
universally conserved element

00:52:28.390 --> 00:52:29.980 align:middle line:90%
like ribosomal RNA.

00:52:29.980 --> 00:52:33.910 align:middle line:84%
But there have been
efforts to look

00:52:33.910 --> 00:52:36.523 align:middle line:84%
for what's present in the
human transcriptome that's not

00:52:36.523 --> 00:52:37.690 align:middle line:90%
present in the human genome.

00:52:37.690 --> 00:52:42.070 align:middle line:84%
The human genome is something
highly purified and cloned

00:52:42.070 --> 00:52:44.170 align:middle line:84%
and so forth, while
the transcriptome

00:52:44.170 --> 00:52:48.550 align:middle line:84%
is whatever cells they
ground up that day and they.

00:52:48.550 --> 00:52:52.810 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, lurking in there
are many of these hepatitis,

00:52:52.810 --> 00:52:57.730 align:middle line:84%
papilloma, Epstein-Barr virus,
retroviral-like elements which

00:52:57.730 --> 00:53:00.280 align:middle line:84%
are not present in the
human genome as yet.

00:53:00.280 --> 00:53:02.390 align:middle line:84%
Human genome is not
completely finished.

00:53:02.390 --> 00:53:04.330 align:middle line:84%
So that's still
an escape clause.

00:53:04.330 --> 00:53:10.660 align:middle line:84%
But basically, these are smoking
guns for at least commensals.

00:53:10.660 --> 00:53:15.760 align:middle line:84%
Microorganisms and viruses that
are living in tissues, some

00:53:15.760 --> 00:53:17.620 align:middle line:90%
of them may be tissue-specific.

00:53:17.620 --> 00:53:20.343 align:middle line:84%
Some of them, subset, may
actually cause disease.

00:53:20.343 --> 00:53:21.760 align:middle line:84%
And that's the
whole problem then,

00:53:21.760 --> 00:53:23.510 align:middle line:90%
is sorting out cause and effect.

00:53:23.510 --> 00:53:27.460 align:middle line:84%
We know how it's been
done, heroically,

00:53:27.460 --> 00:53:28.960 align:middle line:84%
with Helicobacter
pylori, but how do

00:53:28.960 --> 00:53:30.168 align:middle line:90%
we do it with the other ones?

00:53:30.168 --> 00:53:32.125 align:middle line:84%
Some of them will have
tissue-culture models.

00:53:32.125 --> 00:53:36.430 align:middle line:90%


00:53:36.430 --> 00:53:41.110 align:middle line:84%
At the time this slide
was done, but so--

00:53:41.110 --> 00:53:45.790 align:middle line:84%
this is just illustrating
kind of the flow

00:53:45.790 --> 00:53:52.030 align:middle line:84%
of nucleic acid information
that comes in about--

00:53:52.030 --> 00:53:55.420 align:middle line:84%
that was capable of being
mined for new microorganisms,

00:53:55.420 --> 00:53:58.420 align:middle line:84%
new viruses that might be
present in a tissue-specific

00:53:58.420 --> 00:54:01.250 align:middle line:90%
fashion, might be pathogens.

00:54:01.250 --> 00:54:03.370 align:middle line:84%
These are some of the
most popular sequences

00:54:03.370 --> 00:54:04.810 align:middle line:90%
from which we're mining.

00:54:04.810 --> 00:54:09.520 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, human was
popular at times [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:09.520 --> 00:54:11.240 align:middle line:90%
the human genome was in place.

00:54:11.240 --> 00:54:13.000 align:middle line:84%
Here's Brachionus,
our friend, which

00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:15.730 align:middle line:84%
was pretty high up on this
list, considering most of you

00:54:15.730 --> 00:54:18.200 align:middle line:84%
probably didn't hear about
Brachionus before this lecture.

00:54:18.200 --> 00:54:19.990 align:middle line:90%
Now you've heard of it twice.

00:54:19.990 --> 00:54:23.440 align:middle line:84%
There just was a spurt
in sequencing interest.

00:54:23.440 --> 00:54:25.930 align:middle line:84%
And of course, HIV
is the winner year

00:54:25.930 --> 00:54:29.080 align:middle line:84%
after year because of the
importance of resequencing it

00:54:29.080 --> 00:54:31.400 align:middle line:90%
for new mutations.

00:54:31.400 --> 00:54:35.920 align:middle line:84%
And it really has a record
number of new mutations.

00:54:35.920 --> 00:54:43.120 align:middle line:84%
And we've talked about
HIV from a variety

00:54:43.120 --> 00:54:45.400 align:middle line:90%
of different standpoints.

00:54:45.400 --> 00:54:48.280 align:middle line:84%
One of them is
polymerase, is protease,

00:54:48.280 --> 00:54:52.720 align:middle line:84%
is drug targets, and as a
source of drug resistance.

00:54:52.720 --> 00:54:56.550 align:middle line:84%
And that you can follow
up that model, that sort

00:54:56.550 --> 00:54:59.470 align:middle line:84%
of atomic-scale
protein modeling,

00:54:59.470 --> 00:55:04.090 align:middle line:84%
where you're looking
for new drugs

00:55:04.090 --> 00:55:06.250 align:middle line:84%
where the polymerase,
the mutant polymerase,

00:55:06.250 --> 00:55:08.260 align:middle line:84%
will not be resistant
to the new drug.

00:55:08.260 --> 00:55:10.600 align:middle line:84%
But you can also monitor
at the population scale.

00:55:10.600 --> 00:55:13.990 align:middle line:84%
As HIV goes to a
particular patient,

00:55:13.990 --> 00:55:20.530 align:middle line:84%
or to a population of patients,
how does the drug resistance

00:55:20.530 --> 00:55:22.180 align:middle line:90%
change as a function of time?

00:55:22.180 --> 00:55:25.840 align:middle line:84%
Here, the horizontal axis
is in days, up to 30 days,

00:55:25.840 --> 00:55:35.430 align:middle line:84%
and then the vertical
axis is the titers

00:55:35.430 --> 00:55:40.120 align:middle line:84%
of the viruses [INAUDIBLE]
as a function of time.

00:55:40.120 --> 00:55:48.770 align:middle line:84%
And what you have here is
rates of exponential increase.

00:55:48.770 --> 00:55:51.500 align:middle line:84%
These are all on
logarithmic scales.

00:55:51.500 --> 00:55:54.020 align:middle line:84%
And where you're
modeling such a thing

00:55:54.020 --> 00:55:57.643 align:middle line:84%
as the clearance to the
immune system and so forth.

00:55:57.643 --> 00:55:59.060 align:middle line:84%
Originally, this
virus was thought

00:55:59.060 --> 00:56:06.020 align:middle line:84%
to be a very slowly replicating
virus, almost cryptic.

00:56:06.020 --> 00:56:07.400 align:middle line:84%
And then later
was found out this

00:56:07.400 --> 00:56:10.640 align:middle line:84%
is actually a very rapidly
replicating, but immune system

00:56:10.640 --> 00:56:13.817 align:middle line:90%
is very rapidly responding.

00:56:13.817 --> 00:56:15.650 align:middle line:84%
Just each of these
models we've gone through

00:56:15.650 --> 00:56:17.910 align:middle line:84%
has a set of parameters
that it goes along with.

00:56:17.910 --> 00:56:19.610 align:middle line:84%
In a certain sense,
there's quite a bit

00:56:19.610 --> 00:56:22.670 align:middle line:84%
you can learn about a model just
by seeing what parameters are

00:56:22.670 --> 00:56:26.300 align:middle line:84%
present or absent, whether
they're experimentally

00:56:26.300 --> 00:56:28.820 align:middle line:84%
determined, how
accurately, and so on.

00:56:28.820 --> 00:56:33.680 align:middle line:84%
And model parameters here are
mortality rate of uninfected

00:56:33.680 --> 00:56:37.915 align:middle line:84%
CD4+ T-cells, same thing
for infected cells,

00:56:37.915 --> 00:56:40.040 align:middle line:84%
and they can have different
[INAUDIBLE] you can see

00:56:40.040 --> 00:56:44.300 align:middle line:84%
on the far right that there's a
huge difference between a diet

00:56:44.300 --> 00:56:50.250 align:middle line:84%
of 1/4 per day versus
[INAUDIBLE] and so on.

00:56:50.250 --> 00:56:55.250 align:middle line:84%
There's rates for getting
infected, rates of virus loss,

00:56:55.250 --> 00:57:05.900 align:middle line:84%
production, threshold value for
remission, so on and so forth.

00:57:05.900 --> 00:57:09.250 align:middle line:84%
And these are
important parameters

00:57:09.250 --> 00:57:13.540 align:middle line:84%
for how the virus
population changes

00:57:13.540 --> 00:57:15.808 align:middle line:84%
within a person
or the population.

00:57:15.808 --> 00:57:17.350 align:middle line:84%
How it spreads
through the population

00:57:17.350 --> 00:57:24.560 align:middle line:84%
depends on, in many cases, on
the herd immunity and so forth.

00:57:24.560 --> 00:57:27.550 align:middle line:84%
And we get into issues
of public health.

00:57:27.550 --> 00:57:30.870 align:middle line:90%


00:57:30.870 --> 00:57:40.110 align:middle line:84%
It's almost a truism that
most of the additional quality

00:57:40.110 --> 00:57:43.770 align:middle line:84%
years of life, quality-adjusted
life years here,

00:57:43.770 --> 00:57:48.960 align:middle line:84%
QALY, that we have in the
world the fact that our life

00:57:48.960 --> 00:57:50.730 align:middle line:84%
expectancy is so much
longer and so forth,

00:57:50.730 --> 00:57:55.260 align:middle line:84%
are mainly public health
decisions that have been made,

00:57:55.260 --> 00:57:57.060 align:middle line:90%
not so much pharmaceutical.

00:57:57.060 --> 00:58:01.040 align:middle line:90%


00:58:01.040 --> 00:58:08.100 align:middle line:84%
Such things as clean water
have made a huge impact.

00:58:08.100 --> 00:58:09.560 align:middle line:90%
And so we need to think--

00:58:09.560 --> 00:58:12.455 align:middle line:84%
but even when you think about
the pharmaceutical aspects,

00:58:12.455 --> 00:58:14.580 align:middle line:84%
it needs to be thought of
in a public health sense.

00:58:14.580 --> 00:58:18.050 align:middle line:84%
And a surprising amount
of public health officials

00:58:18.050 --> 00:58:23.300 align:middle line:84%
in even the most developed
nations, maybe most of them

00:58:23.300 --> 00:58:25.640 align:middle line:84%
do not have formal
education in public health.

00:58:25.640 --> 00:58:30.320 align:middle line:84%
And so I urge you all to get at
least some education in this.

00:58:30.320 --> 00:58:32.480 align:middle line:90%
Because in this, we have--

00:58:32.480 --> 00:58:36.050 align:middle line:84%
here, they're trying
to build and have

00:58:36.050 --> 00:58:41.220 align:middle line:84%
built quantitative models,
not only for the kinetics

00:58:41.220 --> 00:58:43.880 align:middle line:84%
which a disease will go
through a population or even

00:58:43.880 --> 00:58:50.400 align:middle line:84%
through an individual, but the
way that you make decisions.

00:58:50.400 --> 00:58:53.060 align:middle line:84%
And this is having an impact
on the way decisions are--

00:58:53.060 --> 00:58:55.580 align:middle line:84%
how these different
projects are prioritized.

00:58:55.580 --> 00:58:57.320 align:middle line:84%
At any given time,
you might have

00:58:57.320 --> 00:59:01.820 align:middle line:84%
hundreds of different
candidate vaccines, which

00:59:01.820 --> 00:59:05.810 align:middle line:84%
is one of the major, very
effective public health

00:59:05.810 --> 00:59:08.300 align:middle line:90%
strategies.

00:59:08.300 --> 00:59:10.350 align:middle line:84%
And this is the way
they prioritize it.

00:59:10.350 --> 00:59:14.030 align:middle line:84%
So the level one, saves
money and it improves life.

00:59:14.030 --> 00:59:17.750 align:middle line:90%
So that's almost a no-brainer.

00:59:17.750 --> 00:59:19.140 align:middle line:90%
Then you have different levels.

00:59:19.140 --> 00:59:24.350 align:middle line:84%
We have it might cost
a $10,000, or $100,000

00:59:24.350 --> 00:59:26.915 align:middle line:84%
per quality-adjusted
life year saved.

00:59:26.915 --> 00:59:30.200 align:middle line:90%


00:59:30.200 --> 00:59:33.860 align:middle line:84%
And so the level one
candidates are cytomegalovirus,

00:59:33.860 --> 00:59:36.260 align:middle line:90%
and therapeutic vaccines.

00:59:36.260 --> 00:59:38.720 align:middle line:84%
These are not aimed at
an infectious disease,

00:59:38.720 --> 00:59:42.500 align:middle line:84%
they're for diabetes, rheumatoid
arthritis, multiple sclerosis,

00:59:42.500 --> 00:59:44.195 align:middle line:90%
various bacterial ones.

00:59:44.195 --> 00:59:46.070 align:middle line:84%
And of course, HIV was
not even in this study

00:59:46.070 --> 00:59:50.030 align:middle line:84%
because it was such a
high priority already

00:59:50.030 --> 00:59:50.750 align:middle line:90%
within the NIH.

00:59:50.750 --> 00:59:55.773 align:middle line:90%


00:59:55.773 --> 00:59:58.190 align:middle line:84%
So is there a role for genomics
and computational biology,

00:59:58.190 --> 01:00:01.280 align:middle line:84%
the title of this course,
in this vaccine research

01:00:01.280 --> 01:00:02.840 align:middle line:90%
and development?

01:00:02.840 --> 01:00:05.090 align:middle line:84%
I think the answer
is yes, but it

01:00:05.090 --> 01:00:07.940 align:middle line:84%
requires a great deal of
creativity and resourcefulness

01:00:07.940 --> 01:00:10.130 align:middle line:90%
on all of your parts.

01:00:10.130 --> 01:00:13.730 align:middle line:84%
There are new opportunities
with DNA vaccines where

01:00:13.730 --> 01:00:18.020 align:middle line:84%
you can have one or more
DNAs shot intramuscularly

01:00:18.020 --> 01:00:20.750 align:middle line:90%
or a variety of other ways.

01:00:20.750 --> 01:00:23.000 align:middle line:84%
What is delivered
by the DNA vaccine

01:00:23.000 --> 01:00:27.740 align:middle line:84%
can be various so-called
intracellular vaccines,

01:00:27.740 --> 01:00:30.110 align:middle line:84%
which can either act through
cell-mediated immunity

01:00:30.110 --> 01:00:32.990 align:middle line:84%
or in some other
way intracellularly.

01:00:32.990 --> 01:00:41.480 align:middle line:84%
RNAi is a rapidly emerging
way of delivering things

01:00:41.480 --> 01:00:44.300 align:middle line:84%
that may not be
classically considered

01:00:44.300 --> 01:00:47.270 align:middle line:90%
therapeutics or vaccines.

01:00:47.270 --> 01:00:49.760 align:middle line:90%
The concept of multiplexing--

01:00:49.760 --> 01:00:57.440 align:middle line:84%
many of us have received
multiple vaccines at a time.

01:00:57.440 --> 01:00:59.330 align:middle line:84%
Typically, every
year, your flu vaccine

01:00:59.330 --> 01:01:01.170 align:middle line:84%
will have two or
three strains in it.

01:01:01.170 --> 01:01:06.020 align:middle line:84%
But as you can see from
this middle article,

01:01:06.020 --> 01:01:07.880 align:middle line:84%
the diversity of
certain diseases,

01:01:07.880 --> 01:01:13.340 align:middle line:84%
like HIV and influenza, almost
demand attention from genomics.

01:01:13.340 --> 01:01:16.760 align:middle line:84%
The diversity that can occur,
either from year to year

01:01:16.760 --> 01:01:25.050 align:middle line:84%
or throughout the entire
global set of viruses.

01:01:25.050 --> 01:01:27.740 align:middle line:84%
Another opportunity
is when we have

01:01:27.740 --> 01:01:32.120 align:middle line:84%
vectors for, say,
arthropod vectors,

01:01:32.120 --> 01:01:34.070 align:middle line:90%
insect vectors like mosquito.

01:01:34.070 --> 01:01:40.070 align:middle line:84%
And we have opportunities
to hit malaria and all

01:01:40.070 --> 01:01:41.417 align:middle line:90%
the different life stages.

01:01:41.417 --> 01:01:43.250 align:middle line:84%
There are multiple life
stages within humans

01:01:43.250 --> 01:01:48.440 align:middle line:84%
and multiple stages
within the insect vector.

01:01:48.440 --> 01:01:50.720 align:middle line:84%
And now, the genomics
of these two just

01:01:50.720 --> 01:01:56.750 align:middle line:84%
came out recently, in the same
week, in Nature and Science.

01:01:56.750 --> 01:02:01.517 align:middle line:84%
This provides a whole new
set of inspirations for work

01:02:01.517 --> 01:02:02.600 align:middle line:90%
that can be done on these.

01:02:02.600 --> 01:02:05.000 align:middle line:84%
I think we should take
a little break now,

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:09.250 align:middle line:84%
and we'll wrap up the
talk after that break.